 First, notice $2x-4 \equiv 0 \pmod{2x-4})$, so since this factor has a coefficient with a prime factor, we can solve the equation mod 2}
Notice that the polynomial is already in the same format as a modular arithmetic polynomial. We can subtract 11 $(which will be the remainder when $p(x) = 3x^7-x^6-7x^5+2x^3+4x^2-11$ is divided by a linear polynomial, and notice multiples of the divisor cannot contribute to the remainders)
So we have
\begin{align*}
3x^7-x^6-7x^5+2x^3+4x^2-11 \pmod 2 \\
3x^7-x^6-7x^5+2x^3+4x^2-11\\
3x^7+x^6-3x^5-2x^3+0x^2-11\\
2(3x^7-2x^3-6x^2+12x-24)\end{align*}
Subtracting this from $p(x)$, we have $9x^2-17\equiv 7\pmod{2x-4})$

