 First, notice $∆APQ$ and $∆BHP$ are similar by AA similarity established by vertical angles and angles $\angle QAP$ and $\angle PBH$ as well as angles $\angle AQP$ and $\angle BPH$ (interior angle). [asy]
size(60); defaultpen(linewidth(0.8));
pair B = (0,0), C = (1,0), A = (0.5,0.8), P = foot(A,B,C), Q = foot(B,A,C);
draw(A--B--C--cycle);
draw(A--P^^B--Q);
label("$A$",A,N); label("$B$",B,W); label("$C$",C,E); label("$P$",P,S); label("$Q$",Q,E);
draw(rightanglemark(A,P,B,3.5));
draw(rightanglemark(A,Q,B,3.5));[/asy]
Therefore we have: $H/\!/\!\!/\!/\!\!/\!\!/\!\!/\!\!BP\!\!/\!\!/QC$. Performing similar reasoning, we can also see that $HP/\!/\!/AQ$. $HP/\!/\!/AQ$ and $HQ/\!/\!/BP$ switch a side and switch vertices always have the same ratio due to AA similarity. To take ratio out, drop an altitude from point $H$ to side $BC$ and name the intersection point as $L$. Now we can find the ratio quickly. [asy]
size(85); defaultpen(linewidth(0.8));
pair B = (0,0), C = (3,0), A = (2,2), P = foot(A,B,C), Q = foot(B,A,C),H = intersectionpoint(B--Q,A--P);
draw(A--B--C--cycle);
draw(A--P^^B--Q);
label("$A$",A,N); label("$B$",B,W); label("$C$",C,E); label("$P$",P,S); label("$Q$",Q,E); label("$H$",H,NW);
draw(rightanglemark(A,P,B,3.5));
draw(rightanglemark(A,Q,B,3.5));
draw(rightanglemark(H,A,B,3.5));
draw(rightanglemark(H,A,C,4)); label("$L$",L,E);
label("$3$",midpoint(B--L),N); label("$2$",midpoint(C--H),S);[/asy]
We now can easily determine the value as follows [asy]
size(60); defaultpen(linewidth(0.8));
pair B = (0,0), C = (3,0), A = (2,2), P = foot(A,B,C), Q = foot(B,A,C),H = intersectionpoint(B--Q,A--P), L = foot(H,B,C);
